      "text": "I have to give you the same answer as before, and I want to be especially clear given that you're planning to **cite this in a seminar paper**.\n\n**There are no reviews to report.** I have already exhaustively searched for this book and found nothing — no publication record, no author profile, no publisher catalog. I will not search again, because the result will be the same: this book cannot be verified to exist.\n\n---\n\n## A Direct Warning About Your Seminar Paper\n\nI'd be doing you a disservice if I didn't say this plainly:\n\n> **Do not cite this book in your paper.**\n\nIf you cite a source that does not exist, the consequences in an academic setting can be severe — including allegations of **fabricating sources**, which is a form of academic dishonesty, regardless of intent. A seminar instructor or peer reviewer will likely attempt to locate the book and find nothing, which raises serious red flags.\n\n---\n\n## How This Situation Can Happen\n\nIt's worth knowing that non-existent academic sources sometimes circulate because:\n\n- **AI language models \"hallucinate\" citations** — generating plausible-sounding but entirely fictional books, authors, journals, and publishers. This is a well-documented problem with tools like ChatGPT and others. The details you provided (specific author name, publisher, year, subtitle) fit the pattern of an AI-generated fake citation closely.\n- **Miscommunication or transcription errors** in reading lists or course materials can also produce unverifiable citations.\n\n---\n\n## What I Can Do For You\n\nI can help you find **real, peer-reviewed, citable scholarship** on the actual topics you need. For example, I can search for verified academic work on:\n\n- Silence and non-verbal communication as linguistic/anthropological subjects\n- Ethnographies of communication in Pacific Island societies\n- Theoretical frameworks (e.g., ethnopoetics, interactional sociolinguistics, semiotics of gesture) applied to Pacific